{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>A mamzer</b> — a term for a person estranged from his brothers and acquaintances, so that it is not known whence he comes. This is like the language of וְיָשַׁב מַמְזֵר בְּאַשְׁדּוֹד וְהִכְרַתִּי גְּאוֹן פְּלִשְׁתִּים (Zechariah 9:6) — he says that no one shall dwell in Ashdod but a foreign and alien man who passes through it, for He will cut off the pride of the Philistines from it. Now, one born to a man from his father's wife — the father will distance him and not acknowledge him, because of his shame and disgrace over the evil deed he committed, and his mother too will cast him out at night, like the matter of the foundling mentioned in the Talmud (Kiddushin 69a), and so he is a mamzer in the city of his birth. And likewise all those born of forbidden relations, according to the words of our Sages in the Jerusalem Talmud in Kiddushin (3:14), where Rabbi Abbahu said: What is a mamzer? A foreign blemish (מום זר)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The Hebrew text discusses the etymology of ממזר from זר (foreign/alien), citing the wordplay in the verse and in Rabbi Abbahu's statement. The argument is that the term fundamentally denotes social estrangement rather than a technical legal category, and this sense then extends to all children of forbidden unions."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:15:02.078660377Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}